m200-compare.sh -- tap the mirror port, capture, run corpus_check/scene-on-wire.py/
recover-scene.py/pace-compare.sh, and diff a real M-200's master traffic against
reac-pw's own (wire_census/group_map_scan/ctrl_delta) into m200-vs-reac-pw.md.
Subcommands: tap | capture | analyse | diff | all. `--help` is the usage banner
at the top of the file; the fixture pair to try it on is two captures of an
M-200 with an S-1608 at 48k (the real desk, and ours).

peer-replay.sh, peer_replay.c, peer-replay-compare.py -- the peer-binding proof against
the recorded sessions (make peer-replay / peer-attack / peer-sabotage). Every capture is
replayed frame by frame through two builds (BASE, default origin/main, and this tree);
tests/peer-replay-expected.txt lists each difference with the rule that explains it, and
any other difference is red. The attack arm forges frames from three captures into the
cases the peer binding refuses; the sabotage arm puts each check back and requires both
arms to go red. peer-replay-selftest.sh proves the comparison refuses what nothing
explains and runs in make test. Needs the capture corpus (REAC_CAPTURES).
